    "question": {
      ":question/id": "8dc19d3a-554f-5c43-8d25-f7e2c02d35ed",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2 e^{- \\frac{y}{2}},\\,x>0.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $y=2 \\ln{\\left(x \\right)} - 1$\n- **II.** $y=2 \\ln{\\left(x \\right)}$\n- **III.** $y=- 2 \\ln{\\left(x \\right)} - 1$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the stated domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=2 \\ln{\\left(x \\right)} - 1,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2 e^{- \\frac{y}{2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}2e^{-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\left(2 \\ln{\\left(x \\right)} - 1\\right)} \\\\ \\frac{2}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}2e^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\cdot e^{\\ln\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)} \\\\ \\frac{2}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2 e^{\\frac{1}{2}}}{x} \\\\ \\frac{2 \\left(1 - e^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\right)}{x} & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=2 \\ln{\\left(x \\right)} - 1$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=2 \\ln{\\left(x \\right)},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2 e^{- \\frac{y}{2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}2e^{-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\left(2 \\ln{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)} \\\\ \\frac{2}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}2e^{0}\\cdot e^{\\ln\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)} \\\\ \\frac{2}{x} & =\\frac{2}{x}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=2 \\ln{\\left(x \\right)}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=- 2 \\ln{\\left(x \\right)} - 1,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=- \\frac{2}{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2 e^{- \\frac{y}{2}} \\\\ - \\frac{2}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}2e^{-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\left(- 2 \\ln{\\left(x \\right)} - 1\\right)} \\\\ - \\frac{2}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}2e^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\cdot e^{\\ln\\left(x\\right)} \\\\ - \\frac{2}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}2 x e^{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\ - 2 x e^{\\frac{1}{2}} - \\frac{2}{x} & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=- 2 \\ln{\\left(x \\right)} - 1$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
